Share System catalog colours, because a fresh autoreleased entry per call is a lifetime bug.

+[NSColor controlTextColor] on macOS answers the SAME instance every time and that instance is
never deallocated, so an application may keep the pointer without retaining it, and shipping
applications do exactly that. This minted a NEW autoreleased NSColor_catalog on every call.

Qt asks for its palette colours inside a QMacAutoReleasePool in QCoreApplicationPrivate::init and
keeps them. At the drain the entry it kept was deallocated. Later, something sent
colorUsingColorSpaceName:device: to that dead entry: its isa and name pointers still read correctly
because the memory had not been reused, but its _color slot pointed at a block libobjc HAD reused,
as the class_rw_t of O2Paint. That is why this read like a colour bug for days when it was a
lifetime bug, and why the receiver class differed between runs.

Measured, not reasoned: a one pointer watch on the resolved colour printed two releases at the same
pool drain, the first from -[NSColor_catalog dealloc] and the second from the pool itself. Both are
CORRECT. The colour was supposed to die there. The catalog entry was not supposed to be gone.

System only. Another catalog belongs to the application, which may reuse a system name and may
expect a fresh entry.

With this in, the CMake.app fault moves out of objc_msgSend entirely and into
QMacCGContext::initialize(QPaintDevice*) with CR2 0x24, a null dereference, which is a different
and later defect. Task #227.

